.\" Man page for shared-terminal
.TH SHARED-TERMINAL 1 "November 2026" "shared-terminal 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shared-terminal \- shared local or SSH terminal for a person and a coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shared-terminal
.B --local
.br
.B shared-terminal
.I destination
[
.B --
.I ssh-options...
]
.br
.B shared-terminal
.I command
[
.I options
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shared-terminal
starts a visible terminal UI in the foreground. Use
.B --local
to run your configured interactive shell, or provide an SSH destination to
connect to a remote host. A person types into the terminal directly. A coding
agent connected through the MCP server can inspect recent terminal output and
send input into the same session. Agent input and command output are visible
in the UI.
.PP
For remote sessions, the application uses the local
.BR ssh (1)
client and its normal SSH configuration, identities, and host key checks. It
does not store SSH credentials. Start the visible UI before using the local
control commands or MCP tools. The UI and MCP server must run as the same local
user on the same machine.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI read " [--lines N]"
Print recent output from the active shared session. The default is 120 lines.
.TP
.BI send " TEXT"
Type
.I TEXT
into the active shell and press Enter. This runs locally or remotely, depending
on the session type, and is visible in the UI.
.TP
.B status
Check whether the shared terminal UI is running.
.TP
.B mcp
Run the MCP stdio server. This is normally launched by Codex after MCP
registration; it does not open an SSH session itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --help
Show CLI help. Subcommands have their own help, for example
.BR "shared-terminal read --help" .
.TP
.B --version
Show the installed version.
.TP
.B --
With an SSH destination, pass all following arguments directly to
.BR ssh (1).
.TP
.B --local
Start the user's local shell in the shared terminal instead of connecting over
SSH. Agent commands run locally as the user running the UI.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Send an interrupt to the active process.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Exit the UI and close its shell or SSH process.
.TP
.B Page Up / Page Down
Scroll the displayed terminal.
.TP
.B F12
Exit the UI and close its shell or SSH process.
.SH MCP TOOLS
The MCP server exposes three tools. They work with either a local shell or an
SSH session:
.TP
.B session_status
Check that the user's visible terminal session is running.
.TP
.B terminal_read
Read recent output, up to 120 lines, from the active session.
.TP
.B terminal_send
Type supplied text into the active shell and press Enter. Commands run locally
in local mode or remotely in SSH mode; the user can see the input and output
in the UI.
.PP
The agent and user share the session. Keep the TUI open while using these
tools. The MCP server cannot start or reconnect a session and does not provide
a chat interface.
.SH INSTALLATION
From the repository root, run the installer to build and force-update the
executable, install this man page, and register or refresh the Codex MCP entry:
.PP
.nf
./install.sh
.fi
.PP
The installer skips MCP registration if the Codex CLI is not installed. To
register the server manually, use:
.PP
.nf
codex mcp add shared-terminal -- "$HOME/.cargo/bin/shared-terminal" mcp
.fi
.PP
After changing the code, run
.B ./install.sh
again. It force-updates the binary and removes and recreates the MCP entry,
because the Codex CLI has no force option for
.BR "mcp add" .
Restart Codex and start a new shared-terminal session after installation. To
install this man page without running the installer, use:
.PP
.nf
install -Dm644 man/shared-terminal.1 ~/.local/share/man/man1/shared-terminal.1
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a local shell:
.PP
.nf
shared-terminal --local
.fi
.PP
Start an SSH session:
.PP
.nf
shared-terminal root@server
.fi
.PP
Pass SSH options:
.PP
.nf
shared-terminal root@server -- -p 2222 -i ~/.ssh/work_key
.fi
.PP
Inspect or interact with an active session:
.PP
.nf
shared-terminal status
shared-terminal read --lines 40
shared-terminal send 'uptime'
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1)
.br
Codex MCP guide: https://developers.openai.com/codex/mcp/
.SH LIMITATIONS
This is a local, single-user bridge for one TUI session. It does not manage
multiple sessions or retain a durable transcript after the UI exits.
